+
+Local mapped-memory lifetime fix: real buffer/object destruction now holds the
+recursive object lock through driver release and unlinking. Queue submissions
+scan all mapped objects, including otherwise unrelated buffers; concurrent
+destruction could previously free or clear their mappings during a copy. The
+CPU-only `device-lifetime` fake-driver regression fails against the original
+source and passes with the fix.
+
+Unused Vulkan query pools are now recreated from their creation recipe, allowing
+Qt RHI applications that allocate timestamp pools without using them. Recording
+query commands or reading results still marks the device non-migratable; query
+state/results are not preserved. `tests/test_query_pool.py SOURCE TARGET` checks
+both the unused-pool round trip and refusal after recording a reset command,
+without submitting any GPU work. `zss-run` clears child PRIME/Mesa device filters
+so a session-wide preference cannot hide the destination GPU. Use `--on` or
+`ZSS_START_ON` to select the initial virtual GPU.
+
+Buffer views now retain their buffer dependency and creation chain, recreate real
+view handles, and retain/replay uniform and storage texel descriptors (including
+copies, templates and null entries). A view-usage override requires native
+maintenance5 on the target; unsupported target creation fails for rollback.
+Storage bindings are revisited at submitted draws, including reusable and
+secondary command buffers, rather than only when descriptors are updated. A
+bind-only submission does not mark its storage buffer GPU-written. Writable
+access remains conservatively whole-resource: arbitrary concurrent host/GPU
+access patterns are not claimed as fully coherent by this prototype.
+
+`meson test -C build buffer-view` uses fake driver callbacks with no GPU or
+daemon. `tests/test_texel_buffers.py SOURCE TARGET` explicitly runs six
+one-fragment submissions on two real GPUs, verifies byte-exact shader-written
+values through both moves, and reuses descriptors/templates, a mapped pointer
+and a command buffer. It never detaches hardware or changes power policy.
